(vector (map (:db/id -1)
  (:adr/id "ADR-2608196000")
  (:adr/title "The generalised scheduler was not installed either")
  (:adr/status "accepted")
  (:adr/date "2026-08-19")
  (:adr/supersedes "")
  (:adr/body
"## What was measured

ADR-2608124800 found roughly twenty verification scripts that read `orgs/` and
therefore cannot be murakumo fleet gates -- a node gets only the target repo's
tree, so a detector that scans `orgs/` finds an empty workspace and can never go
green. `root-permit-index` stood at 0 pass / 282 fail for exactly that.

Its diagnosis is quoted in `scripts/orgs-detector-tick.cljs`'s own header:

    That home existed as five uninstalled launchd plists in `scripts/` and was
    never loaded; one of them was the only caller of three verifiers, one of
    which guards custody that has already cost irreversible asset loss.
    **Nothing was wrong with the checks. The scheduler was never installed.**

So it built one registry-driven tick, `so the next detector is a registry entry
rather than a sixth plist that also does not get installed`.

Measured 2026-08-19: **that tick had no LaunchAgent either.**

  - `ls ~/Library/LaunchAgents | grep -i detector` -- nothing
  - `crontab -l | grep detector` -- nothing
  - no plist anywhere in the workspace names `orgs-detector-tick`
  - `~/.gftd/orgs-detector-tick/state.edn` last updated 2026-08-18T06:55Z,
    about 32 hours earlier

Fifteen detectors are registered against it. Run by hand, the tick works: it
respects per-detector intervals, resolves findings, and writes state. Nothing
was wrong with the checks. The scheduler was never installed -- again, and to
the thing whose header says so.

## Why it stayed invisible

The SessionStart hook already computes per-detector staleness and prints
`最終測定 4d前`. That reads as *nothing changed*, not as *nothing ran*, and the
two are indistinguishable from a detector's own line: a detector that has not
been run reports exactly what a detector with no new findings reports.

## Decision

1. **Install the LaunchAgent.** `com.gftd.orgs-detector-tick`, StartInterval
   21600 (six hours -- the shortest per-detector interval; the tick skips what
   is not due), WorkingDirectory at the superproject root, log to
   `~/.gftd/orgs-detector-tick.stdout.log`. Loaded and confirmed in
   `launchctl list`.

2. **Keep the plist in the repository** at
   `scripts/com.gftd.orgs-detector-tick.plist`. This is exactly what the five
   uninstalled plists did, so it is not the fix -- it is how the fix is
   reproducible on another machine.

3. **The hook checks for the scheduler**, and says so louder than any single
   detector's age, because every one of those ages is a consequence of it.
   The absence prints an install command rather than a complaint.

## Evidence

Both directions, run against the real hook: with the plist present the warning
is absent and the report prints; with the plist moved aside, the hook prints
`⚠⚠ この tick に scheduler がありません`. The tick itself was run by hand first
and wrote state -- 58 new vendored-copy findings, 93 resolved shadow-source-path
findings, and a `binding-stale` on the Valueflows projection, none of which had
been reported for 32 hours.

## What this does not fix

The check is for a plist file's existence, not for the job being loaded or
firing. `launchctl unload` leaves the file in place, and the hook would say
nothing. A stronger check would parse `launchctl list`, and it is not written.

There are 34 `verify-*.cljs` scripts in `scripts/`; 15 are registered detectors
and 5 are named by a fleet gate or a hook. **What runs the other 14 was not
measured here.**")))
